<!--
  https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/fundamentals/package-validation/diagnostic-ids

  Every entry here is a deliberate, reviewed decision. A suppression that nobody can explain is a
  silenced bug, so do not add one without recording why below, and delete one the moment the reason
  stops holding. Regenerating this file with -p:ApiCompatGenerateSuppressionFile=true rewrites it and
  DISCARDS these comments - regenerate into a scratch copy and merge by hand instead.

  IEnumerableExtensions.Index<TSource> - present on the net8.0 leg, absent on net10.0.
  Intentional and pre-existing: the method is a polyfill guarded by #if !NET9_0_OR_GREATER,
  because .NET 9 added Enumerable.Index with an identical signature. On net10.0 the consumer gets
  the BCL's, so nothing is lost. Compiling it on both legs would collide exactly the way
  ArrayExtensions.Reverse did against .NET 10's Enumerable.Reverse<TSource>(TSource[]), until 1.0.0
  renamed that family ToReversed - this guard is the pattern that avoids that, not a defect.
  Invisible until the package multi-targeted, because a single-TFM package has no second leg to
  differ from.

  The six ArrayExtensions.Reverse baseline suppressions that covered that rename against 0.7.0 are
  gone: Core's baseline is pinned to 1.0.0, which never had the old names, and ApiCompat fails a
  pack on a suppression it no longer needs. Should the baseline return to the shared 0.7.0
  (bld/Versioning.props), for instance when the out-of-band override and its pinned baseline are
  removed, CP0002 errors for those six overloads are that rename, and their suppressions come back.
-->
<Suppressions xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
  <Suppression>
    <DiagnosticId>CP0002</DiagnosticId>
    <Target>M:Bodu.Collections.Generic.Extensions.IEnumerableExtensions.Index``1(System.Collections.Generic.IEnumerable{``0})</Target>
    <Left>lib/net8.0/Bodu.Core.dll</Left>
    <Right>lib/net10.0/Bodu.Core.dll</Right>
  </Suppression>
</Suppressions>